Subject: [PATCH] avfilter/audio: reinit the link frame pool whenever the request size differs

ff_default_get_audio_buffer() kept a link's frame pool as long as it was
at least as large as the request, so one large request permanently
oversized the pool for every later frame on that link. loudnorm in dynamic
mode triggers exactly that: its first ff_inlink_consume_samples() asks for
3 s at 192 kHz, take_samples() allocates the concatenation buffer from the
inlink pool, and from then on every frame the auto-inserted aresample
pushes onto that link (160 samples for a TrueHD access unit) occupies a
3 s x channels x 8 B buffer -- 35 MiB at 7.1. loudnorm releases nothing
until 100 ms are queued, which takes 120 of those frames: 4 GiB of
mappings on a TV box, then ENOMEM and "Disabling filter loudnorm because
it has failed".

Backport of the audio pool semantics of upstream ad7956d5bb50
("avfilter/{audio,video}: switch to ff_frame_pool_*_reinit()", 2026-03):
a request whose sample count differs from the pool's reinitializes the
pool, so a buffer is only ever as large as the frame that asked for it.
Video pools are untouched. Drop this patch once the pinned FFmpeg carries
that commit.

diff --git a/libavfilter/audio.c b/libavfilter/audio.c
index 7d87e9e..2d34adc 100644
--- a/libavfilter/audio.c
+++ b/libavfilter/audio.c
@@ -67,7 +67,12 @@ AVFrame *ff_default_get_audio_buffer(AVFilterLink *link, int nb_samples)
             return NULL;
         }
 
-        if (pool_channels != channels || pool_nb_samples < nb_samples ||
+        /* Size the pool to the request exactly. A pool that outlives a larger
+         * request (a consumer's ff_inlink_consume_samples() window landing on
+         * this link) would hand that oversized buffer to every later frame,
+         * however small, and a consumer that waits for many such frames before
+         * releasing any holds the whole set at once. */
+        if (pool_channels != channels || pool_nb_samples != nb_samples ||
             pool_format != link->format || pool_align != align) {
 
             ff_frame_pool_uninit(&li->frame_pool);
